## 4. Visual system and art direction — follow exactly

### 4.1 Product-design intent

Design SHARESPACE as a credible institutional operations product used daily by students, lab technicians, resource owners, and administrators. The interface should feel like carefully designed operational software: quiet, precise, dependable, legible, and information-rich. It should not look like a startup landing page, a consumer marketplace, a generic AI-generated SaaS dashboard, or a Dribbble concept.

**Art direction:** restrained editorial utility, inspired by well-designed library systems, university facilities software, and modern professional administration tools. Use strong typographic hierarchy, disciplined alignment, practical tables, clear forms, simple line icons, and real operational detail. Prioritise task completion and decision traceability over decoration.

### 4.2 Hard visual prohibitions

These are non-negotiable. Do not use them anywhere in the application:
- No gradients of any kind: no background gradients, text gradients, gradient buttons, gradient borders, gradient chart fills, or gradient image placeholders.
- No pink + blue, purple + blue, cyan + magenta, neon, vaporwave, or stereotypical “AI product” colour palettes.
- No glassmorphism, frosted-glass panels, glowing borders, blurred colour blobs, aurora backgrounds, floating orbs, mesh gradients, excessive blur, or glowing shadows.
- No generic “bento grid” dashboard made from many equally sized rounded cards. Do not put every piece of information in its own card.
- No oversized hero headline, giant decorative icon, fake AI assistant orb, floating command palette as the main visual feature, or marketing-style headline on the signed-in dashboard.
- No giant pill buttons, excessively rounded cards, bubbly controls, decorative emoji, or random illustrations.
- No arbitrary decorative charts, meaningless metrics, fake live indicators, fake map panels, fake activity just to fill whitespace, or repeated identical KPI cards.
- No Lorem ipsum, “Welcome to your dashboard” filler, generic feature copy, or unexplained jargon.
- No colour-only statuses. No invented trust badges such as “AI verified”, “100% secure”, or “blockchain protected”.
- Do not invent extra screens, features, or visual flourishes not required by this brief.

If uncertain between a decorative choice and a clearer operational layout, choose the clearer layout. Keep the screen visually calm even when the data is dense.

