### 4.4 Typography

- Use `Inter` if already available locally; otherwise use a system stack such as `Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif`. Do not require an external font service for the app to run.
- Base body text: 15–16px, line-height 1.45–1.6. Do not make essential body copy smaller than 14px.
- Page title: 24–28px, weight 650–700. Section title: 17–20px, weight 600. Card/row title: 15–17px, weight 600. Labels: 13–14px, medium weight. Metadata: 13–14px with adequate contrast.
- Use sentence case, not Title Case Everywhere. Avoid oversized headings and excessive bold text.
- Use tabular numerals for time, counts, identifiers, and quantities where supported. Use a monospace font only for hashes, tokens, and technical identifiers.
- Make text hierarchy carry the design. Do not rely on colour or decorative icons to distinguish everything.

### 4.5 Layout, spacing, and component geometry

- Use a consistent 4px base with spacing values primarily in 8px increments. Align labels, controls, tables, and headings to shared edges.
- Desktop app shell: fixed/collapsible left sidebar approximately 236–252px wide; top application bar approximately 60–68px high; content area with a maximum width around 1440px and 24–32px page padding on wide screens.
- Sidebar is a practical navigation rail, not a promotional panel. Keep the wordmark small, active navigation obvious, icons consistent, and the organisation/user switcher near the bottom. Use a thin border between sidebar and content; do not add gradients or ornamental shapes.
- Page headers should contain a clear title, one-line contextual description where useful, breadcrumbs for deeper pages, and one primary action aligned to the right when the workflow warrants it. Never add arbitrary actions simply to balance the layout.
- Prefer tables, structured lists, labelled sections, stepper flows, definition lists, side panels, and compact summary strips over card grids. Use a card only when a group of information genuinely needs a visual boundary.
- Corners: 6px for inputs and small controls; 8px for buttons, menus, and standard panels; 10–12px only for larger dialogs or distinct surfaces. Avoid the default “everything rounded-xl” look.
- Use 1px borders, separators, and at most a subtle shadow for menus/dialogs. Main content panels should generally rely on surface contrast and borders rather than shadows.
- Buttons should be rectangular with modest corner radius. Primary actions are solid forest green; secondary actions are white/transparent with a border; destructive actions use restrained red. Do not use gradients or oversized pill styling.
- Create visual rhythm through whitespace, alignment, type, and dividers—not decorative circles, oversized icons, or empty showcase panels.
- Do not fill every horizontal inch. Let dense data tables be dense enough to scan, but provide comfortable row height and clear column labels.

### 4.6 Application shell and responsive behaviour

- Desktop (1280px and wider): persistent left sidebar, top bar, page title region, then task-specific content. Do not force all pages into a dashboard layout; each screen should use the layout suited to its task.
- Tablet: sidebar can collapse to icon rail or drawer; tables may scroll horizontally with sticky first column when helpful.
- Mobile (375px baseline): compact top bar and a bottom navigation bar for the four most-used destinations, with the rest in an accessible menu sheet. Respect safe-area insets. Do not simply shrink the desktop sidebar or compress a wide table into unreadable cards.
- On mobile, convert tables into clear stacked rows only when all labels remain visible; otherwise allow deliberate horizontal scrolling with a visible affordance. Forms become single-column. Sticky action bars are acceptable only when they do not cover fields or device keyboard.
- Check-in, return, QR scan, condition capture, and incident reporting must feel purpose-built for a phone. Keep the main action reachable, touch targets at least 44×44px, photo previews legible, and critical decisions easy to review before submission.
- At 375px, no horizontal page overflow, clipped labels, overlapping buttons, invisible focus rings, or dialogs wider than the viewport.

### 4.7 Data visualisation

- Use Recharts only for questions the data actually answers. Charts must have useful labels, units, axis ticks, tooltips, and empty states. Provide a text/table equivalent for important information.
- Use a restrained palette from the product tokens. No gradient fills, rainbow series, excessive grid lines, 3D/pie decoration, fake real-time motion, or chart animation that delays task completion.
- Prefer simple bars, lines, and a small heatmap where suitable. Every chart title states what is measured; every insight states the evidence and is labelled as a suggestion, not an automated decision.
- Never show made-up totals that conflict with the seeded records. Counts and charts must derive from the same mock dataset and update when relevant records change.

### 4.8 Imagery and icons

- Use `lucide-react` outline icons with consistent stroke width and size. Use icons to aid recognition; pair them with text for navigation and consequential actions. Do not mix icon families, use emoji as interface icons, or substitute icons for labels.
- No remote image URLs, external image CDNs, or dependency on network access. For resource thumbnails, create a small set of local SVG illustrations or neutral, category-specific placeholders (electronics instrument, camera kit, workstation, room) with solid neutral backgrounds and restrained line art. No gradient placeholders, random stock photos, or repeated generic coloured rectangles.
- On a resource detail page, use a consistent gallery frame with the local category illustration and a factual caption where appropriate. Do not pretend an illustration is a photograph of the actual asset.
- QR codes must be scannable in the generated demo where possible, with a readable asset token beneath each code. A decorative fake QR pattern is not acceptable.

### 4.9 Interaction, motion, and feedback

- Use restrained transitions of approximately 120–180ms for hover, focus, drawers, and small state changes. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; avoid parallax, springy motion, looping animation, confetti, and animated counters.
- Every action that changes data must provide pending, success, and failure feedback. Prevent duplicate submissions. Disable a control only when there is a clear reason, and show that reason next to it or in an accessible tooltip.
- Confirm consequential actions such as policy activation, role changes, cancellation when a fee/commitment applies, archival, and incident resolution. Ask for a reason when the domain rules require one.
- Use clear toast messages for transient confirmation and inline messages for errors that need correction. Do not communicate critical information exclusively through a toast.
- Focus order must follow the visible workflow. Dialogs and drawers trap focus, close predictably, and restore focus to the trigger.

### 4.10 Accessibility and design consistency

- Target WCAG 2.1 AA: semantic landmarks, proper heading order, visible focus indicators, keyboard operability, labelled controls, field errors connected with `aria-describedby`, accessible dialogs/menus with focus management, live regions for toasts, contrast checks, and reduced motion.
- Status always combines readable text + an icon or shape + colour. Never rely on colour alone.
- Forms need labels, help text where needed, field-level validation, and keyboard-friendly date/time selection. Use actual buttons/links for actions/navigation rather than clickable generic containers.
- Provide a complete light theme and dark theme, not merely inverted colours. Persist the user's theme choice and use `prefers-color-scheme` for the initial default.
- Implement reusable primitives: Button, IconButton, Input, Select, Textarea, DateTimePicker, Badge, StatusPill, Card/Panel, Tabs, Dialog, Drawer, Toast, Tooltip, Skeleton, EmptyState, Stepper, Timeline, DataTable (sortable, filterable, paginated), FilterBar, ConfirmDialog (requires reason when needed), FileDropzone, PhotoCapture, ConditionChecklist, PolicyForm, ChainBadge, QRCode, and ErrorBoundary. Keep the primitives visually consistent; do not create a new style for every screen.

### 4.11 Required page composition patterns

Use the following patterns to avoid generic dashboard design:
- **Dashboard:** page header + a compact row of only the most actionable counts + a primary work queue/schedule area + one or two secondary lists. Content changes by role. No bento wall of identical cards.
- **Discover:** search/filter toolbar + clearly switchable list/grid results. Resource rows/cards prioritise name, location, available time, requirements, and eligibility. Keep images secondary to the information.
- **Resource detail:** two-column desktop layout where useful: main details/availability on the left, requirements and request action in a clear right-hand panel. Stack in a meaningful order on mobile.
- **Request flow:** focused stepper, clear time and purpose inputs, a prominent eligibility report with rule-by-rule reasons, and a review summary before submission. Do not decorate it like a checkout funnel.
- **Review workspace:** dense but readable queue table, then a decision page comparing request details, rules, conflicts, and priority factors. Reason entry must be visible before a consequential decision.
- **Check-in/return:** narrow mobile-friendly form with a clear sequence, accessory checklist, condition selector, notes, photo capture, and final review. Do not use decorative cards for every field.
- **Incident review:** explicit reported-allegation notice, evidence and timeline, clear reviewer actions, and a written resolution area. Never visually imply guilt before a human decision.
- **Trust Ledger:** chronological vertical event list/table with actor, time, reason, linked entity, sequence, and hash. Hash verification state should be unambiguous and evidence-based.
- **Policies:** policy table/list + structured editor + “preview against sample” results. Make active version and effective time easy to see.
- **Analytics:** two or three relevant charts with supporting data tables and concise explanations, not a colourful wall of charts.

### 4.12 Final visual acceptance check

Before finishing, inspect every route at desktop width (1440px) and mobile width (375px). Remove any unrequested gradient, generic bento grid, excessive rounded card, decorative metric, placeholder copy, stock-looking illustration, low-contrast text, or dead control. If the interface could be mistaken for a generic AI SaaS template, revise it toward the restrained institutional operations direction defined above.

