## 12. Implementation quality, integrity, and acceptance criteria

### 12.1 Non-negotiable functional quality
- Build a complete app, not a static prototype, one dashboard, or a collection of disconnected screenshots.
- It must compile and run with no TypeScript errors and no browser-console errors on normal navigation. No broken imports, fake route links, silent button failures, or unhandled rejected promises.
- Every visible action must work. If an action cannot be completed in the current mode, explain that in the UI and disable it. Do not leave decorative buttons or pretend-to-work controls.
- Persist mock data across reloads. Changes to resources, policies, bookings, incidents, notifications, and ledger must be reflected in every related screen and derived count.
- Include loading, empty, success, error, and permission-denied states for the relevant components. Avoid indefinite spinners and empty white screens.
- Use the typed API client for domain operations; do not bypass it by mutating page-local arrays. Keep the mock and live API response contracts aligned.
- The client never sets a booking state directly. It invokes the correct action endpoint, receives the updated record, and renders the server/mock-server result.
- Prevent double-click duplicates, overlapping bookings, invalid state transitions, usage-cap bypasses, and cross-organisation data leakage. Enforce these in the mock server, not just in component conditionals.

### 12.2 Truthfulness, trust, and safety
- Do not claim live verification, real payments, real SSO, production compliance, or actual notifications. Clearly label simulations (e.g. “Demo verification”, “Simulated notifications”, “Local demo data”).
- Do not collect or display unnecessary personal data. Use fictional identities and emails only.
- Incident reports are allegations until a human reviewer resolves them. No automatic blame, penalties, guilt language, or automated liability decisions. Any assisted draft must be reviewed and confirmed by a person before submission.
- Allocation suggestions may show deterministic factors and policy version, but the authorised human makes the final decision. Show override reasons and record them in the ledger.
- Use the phrase “tamper-evident history”; do not describe the ledger as immutable or blockchain-based.
- Never make an AI feature required for core use. `VITE_ENABLE_AI` defaults to `false`, and all core journeys must work without an AI service.

### 12.3 Engineering standards
- Use strict TypeScript domain types, typed API functions, small feature-focused components, and shared hooks/utilities. Avoid `any` unless there is a documented boundary and a narrow reason.
- Keep domain logic in `src/mocks/server.ts` and shared domain utilities, not duplicated across components. Isolate deterministic eligibility, allocation order, state transitions, usage caps, tenant checks, and ledger hashing so they can be unit-tested.
- Use stable IDs, UTC ISO timestamps, explicit error types, and predictable error handling. Treat `localStorage` contents as untrusted input: validate loaded state and recover gracefully from malformed or stale demo data.
- Create a reset-demo-data action in the demo/admin settings so reviewers can rerun the seeded journeys. Require confirmation before resetting.
- Use local assets only. The app should start and operate without credentials, external API keys, external image services, or network access beyond development dependencies.
- Add a `README.md` documenting setup, scripts, demo accounts, role switching, mock/live modes, seeded scenarios, known simulations, and backend integration. Add an `/about` page listing implemented, simulated, and planned capabilities accurately.
- Add tests for the booking state machine, eligibility engine, time overlap, rolling usage cap, policy version behavior, waitlist promotion, role/tenant permissions, idempotency, and ledger chain verification. If time prevents a full suite, implement and run the highest-risk tests first and report remaining gaps honestly.

### 12.4 Visual and responsive quality gate
- Follow Section 4 exactly. No gradients, generic bento layouts, pink/blue or purple/blue palette combinations, glassmorphism, neon, giant pill buttons, emoji icons, or decorative AI-themed effects.
- Keep the UI institutional, operational, restrained, and readable. Use the specified forest-green/neutral/ochre palette and CSS tokens. Do not add a new colour system while styling individual screens.
- Inspect at 1440px and 375px. Fix overflow, clipping, unreadable data, non-functional mobile navigation, poorly sized dialogs, and inaccessible controls.
- Ensure every status is communicated through text plus icon/shape plus colour. Verify colour contrast in light and dark themes.
- Each route must look designed for its task; do not force every route into the same card layout. Use tables for operational queues, timelines for history, forms for data entry, and compact charts for analysis.
- Charts, badges, counts, and summary metrics must be derived from seeded records and update when those records change. Do not fabricate metrics or present simulated data as live.

### 12.5 Build and self-review sequence
Implement in this order, without stopping after the first visual shell:
1. Inspect dependencies and establish design tokens, theme handling, shared components, application shell, router, and accessible navigation.
2. Implement demo authentication, role selection, organisation context, typed domain models, seed records, storage validation, and mock API error handling.
3. Implement resource discovery, filtering, resource detail, availability, eligibility preview, and booking request.
4. Implement booking list/detail, manager queue and decision workspace, check-in, return, and staff handover confirmation.
5. Implement incident reporting/review, ledger timeline/hash verification, policy versions/preview/activation, asset QR flows, notifications, analytics, user/role administration, and profile.
6. Connect every screen to the same mock data and API layer; test cross-screen state changes and the organisation boundary.
7. Run the full demo script in Section 11, then inspect every route in light/dark mode at desktop and mobile dimensions. Fix functional, responsive, accessibility, and visual acceptance failures before claiming completion.

If context or generation limits prevent a complete implementation in one pass, do not silently omit screens. Prioritise a compiling architecture and a complete end-to-end booking lifecycle, then implement remaining routes in the listed order. Report precisely what is implemented, what is mocked, and what remains. Do not label the app complete unless the acceptance checks actually pass.

